<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apuleius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 124 – after 170</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apuleius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writer, orator and Platonist philosopher from Madauros in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His novel the Metamorphoses, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-golden-ass">The Golden Ass</a>, tells of a man turned into a donkey and is the only Latin novel to survive complete. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and lectured at Carthage, and his Apologia records his defence against a charge of using magic to win a rich widow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apuleius.jpg" alt="Apuleius" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_philosopher_(probably_Apuleius_or_Lactantius)_on_late_antique_ceiling_painting_from_Trier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pagan Latin literature also owed much to Africa, from <b>Apuleius</b>, author of the Metamorphoses, who lectured at Carthage, to later grammarians and poets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The cult of Isis spread across the Hellenistic and Roman world, with temples from Delos to Pompeii, Rome and the Rhine frontier; the Roman novelist <b>Apuleius</b> described initiation into her mysteries in the 2nd century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Vergil’s Aeneid gave the Imperial vision of the past, Pliny the Elder wrote a sceptical history of magic, and <b>Apuleius</b>’s The Golden Ass almost certainly describes the mysteries of Isis.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Madauros, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Roman Carthage. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.08,7.9&amp;year=153" aria-label="Madauros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4749 861 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Apuleius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5199 1131h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5199 1131h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.08,7.9&amp;year=153">What was here in 153 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
